# Working in `src/language`

Layout:
- `src/language/minab.langium` — the grammar. Hand-written, tracked in git. Mirrors the reference grammar in spec §11 exactly — the two must never drift.
- `src/language/minab-module.ts` — Langium DI module wiring. Currently wires only **core** (non-LSP) services, imported from `langium` (not `langium/lsp`). There is no language server or VS Code extension yet — see `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 7 before assuming one exists.
- `src/language/generated/` — output of `langium generate`. Gitignored. Never hand-edit; regenerate instead.
- `src/language/schema.ts` — the host-supplied schema contract (`MinabSchema`/`SchemaProvider`: tables, columns, built-in function signatures). Minab has no in-file table declarations — this is the only source of truth for what `#alias`/bare table names mean, and it's genuinely per-embedding data, passed into `createMinabServices(context, schema)` rather than hardcoded.
- `src/language/minab-scope-resolver.ts` — `MinabScopeResolver`, resolution for the scope-stack sigils (`.`/`^`/`#alias`/`KEY`, spec §2.2) plus bare `NameRef` lookups. Not a Langium `ScopeProvider`/Linker hookup — see the file's own header comment for why, and `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 2 for the full story. Since Phase 4, also resolves a `.field`-rooted receiver's own target table (`.orders[...]`'s `.orders`, previously punted), via `tableOfExpression`/`followRelationColumn`.
- `src/language/minab-validator.ts` — `MinabValidator`, the Langium `Validator` (Phase 3: `$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement; Phase 4: the full type-checking diagnostic set, built on `minab-type-checker.ts`). See the file's own header comment for the exact check list.
- `src/language/minab-types.ts` — `MinabType` (Phase 4): the structured type representation (`scalar`/`tuple`/`record`/`collection`/`null`) used by both `schema.ts`'s column/function types and `MinabTypeChecker`'s inference output, plus assignability/coercion helpers (`isAssignableTo`, `baseTypesEqual`, `widenNumeric`, ...).
- `src/language/minab-builtins.ts` — the 8 built-in aggregate/predicate function signatures (`COUNT`/`SUM`/`AVG`/`MIN`/`MAX`/`EXISTS`/`ALL`/`ANY`, spec §5.3.1) — reserved names, called bare, never `&`-prefixed.
- `src/language/minab-type-checker.ts` — `MinabTypeChecker` (Phase 4): `inferType(node: Expression)` over the full expression grammar, following `MinabScopeResolver`'s `ScopeResolution`/`{ok,reason}` pattern. See the file's own header comment for the traversal/coercion/null rules it implements.
- `test/parsing.test.ts` — vitest smoke suite, using `parseHelper` from `langium/test`. Structured as one `describe` block per section of `docs/showcase.md` (49 tests as of 2026-09-13) — every construct in the showcase should have a passing parse example here, plus a dedicated block confirming that examples the showcase flags as *semantic* errors still parse cleanly.
- `test/scoping.test.ts` — resolution tests for `MinabScopeResolver` (12 tests as of 2026-09-14), using a small fixture `MinabSchema` rather than a real host schema. Separate from the parsing smoke suite since it asserts on resolution results, not just clean parsing.
- `test/validation.test.ts` — `MinabValidator`'s Phase 3 checks (`$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement), using `validationHelper` from `langium/test` and a fixture `MinabSchema`.
- `test/typechecking.test.ts` — the Phase 4 type-checking diagnostic set, same `validationHelper` pattern, its own broader fixture schema (multiple tables, `ref`/`collection` columns, `JSON`).

Workflow for any grammar change:
1. Edit `src/language/minab.langium`.
2. `npm run langium:generate` — regenerates the AST, grammar, and module in `src/language/generated`. Treat any ambiguity/conflict warning as worth understanding, not just "did it exit 0" — the one existing warning (`Postfix`'s `TupleAccess` vs. `FilterAccess` over a bare `[NUMBER]`) is a known, accepted one (spec §12 item 4), not something to silently add to.
3. `npm run build` — typechecks against the regenerated AST types.
4. Add or update a case in `test/parsing.test.ts` covering the new syntax against `docs/showcase.md`'s own example for it, then `npm test`. **Clean generation and a clean build are not proof the grammar is right** — actually run the parse test. The 2026-09-13 v2 integration passed `langium generate`/`tsc -b` without a single error, and the test suite still caught three real defects (see `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 1): a clause-order regression, a `CurrentRecord` node missing a `vivify` flag that a headline spec example depended on, and an operator misused against an operand its own grammar rule doesn't accept. When something doesn't parse, don't assume it's a Chevrotain lookahead bug before checking the simpler explanation — re-read the exact grammar rule for the operator/construct involved; two of those three turned out to be plain example mistakes once checked against the grammar's own definition, not parser defects.
5. Only after 2-4 are green, and only with the approval described in the spec-governance rule, update `docs/query-language-spec.md` (§11's grammar block, plus whatever prose sections the change affects) and `docs/showcase.md` (a runnable example for the new construct, in its own numbered section, cross-referencing back to the spec section). Section numbers and cross-references need to stay consistent in both files; that's part of the change, not a follow-up.

Debugging a parse failure: when `expectNoErrors`-style assertions fail with a generic "Expecting end of file but found `X`" message, that only tells you *where* the parser gave up, not *why* — a failure deep inside a nested function/loop/if can surface as an error at the very first token of the whole document, because the outer rule's parse attempt aborts entirely and error recovery reports its own entry point. Bisect by extracting the smallest snippet that still fails (a temporary `describe('debug', ...)` block with a few `console.log`-ing test cases works well, deleted once done) before concluding it's a genuine grammar defect rather than a simple misuse of some other construct.

Current implementation status (update this line as work lands, don't let it go stale): full v2 grammar (types, functions, control flow, loops, DML) plus core Langium services, verified against `docs/showcase.md`'s 49-case suite as of 2026-09-13. Sigil resolution (`src/language/minab-scope-resolver.ts`, `MinabScopeResolver`, since 2026-09-14) resolves `.`/`^`/`#alias`/`KEY` (spec §2) against the §2.2 scope stack — still *not* a Langium `ScopeProvider` wired into the Linker, since nothing in the grammar uses cross-reference (`[Type:ID]`) syntax; table/column/function metadata comes from a host-supplied `MinabSchema`/`SchemaProvider` (`src/language/schema.ts`) passed to `createMinabServices`, not in-file declarations. As of 2026-09-16 (Phase 3), a `Validator` (`src/language/minab-validator.ts`) exists and catches `$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement errors. As of 2026-09-17 (Phase 4), a real type system exists: `src/language/minab-types.ts`'s structured `MinabType` replaced `schema.ts`'s free-form column-type string; `src/language/minab-type-checker.ts`'s `MinabTypeChecker` infers types over the full expression grammar (multi-hop `ref` traversal, `collection` broadcast, built-in/user function resolution); and `MinabValidator` now also enforces no-implicit-coercion, the §3.4 collection-vs-scalar boundary, and the §7.7 null-operand rules — backed by `test/typechecking.test.ts`. A program can still be grammatically valid and semantically wrong in ways nothing here catches yet (the execution/write-semantics layer, Phase 5, hasn't been designed) — see `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 5.

Don't add a `ScopeProvider`/`Validator`/other custom service by guessing Langium's current API shape — check the installed `langium` version's types first (`node_modules/langium/lib/**/*.d.ts` or the generated `module.ts` for the exact imports it already uses), since Langium's service surface has moved between major versions (this project is on `langium` ~4.4, core services only).
